## 1.  What is the **Unit‑of‑Work** pattern?

| **Aspect** | **Explanation** |
|------------|-----------------|
| **Purpose** | Coordinate changes to a set of objects so that they can be persisted in a single “unit” of work. |
| **Scope** | A “unit” is typically a business transaction (e.g., “create a new order and update inventory”). |
| **Responsibilities** | 1. **Change Tracking** – remember which objects are new, dirty (modified) or deleted. <br>2. **Identity Map** – keep one in‑memory instance per database row to avoid duplicates. <br>3. **Transactional Cohesion** – flush all changes in a single commit or roll back entirely. |
| **Benefit** | <ul><li>Atomicity (all or nothing)</li><li>Consistent read‑your‑writes (you see the changes you just made)</li><li>Reduced round‑trips to the database (batch flush)</li><li>Clear separation of business logic from persistence plumbing</li></ul> |

> *“Unit‑of‑Work” is a general concept. In JPA/Hibernate the `Session`/`EntityManager` already implements it, but you can still build your own thin wrapper to keep your code agnostic of the ORM.*

---

## 2.  High‑level Java skeleton

```java
public interface UnitOfWork {

    /** Register a brand‑new entity to be persisted. */
    void registerNew(Object entity);

    /** Register an entity that was modified. */
    void registerDirty(Object entity);

    /** Register an entity that will be deleted. */
    void registerDeleted(Object entity);

    /** Persist everything in a single transaction. */
    void commit() throws PersistenceException;

    /** Roll back the transaction. */
    void rollback();
}
```

### Implementation details

1. **EntityManager** – the JPA handle that actually talks to the database.  
2. **Collections** – keep three lists (`newEntities`, `dirtyEntities`, `deletedEntities`).  
3. **Transaction handling** – begin, commit, rollback via `EntityTransaction`.  
4. **Identity map** – not required if you rely on JPA’s `EntityManager`, but we’ll show a simple map for clarity.

---

## 3.  A concrete, minimal example

Below is a **fully working** demo that shows how a `UnitOfWork` can be built on top of JPA/Hibernate.  
(You can paste it into a Maven/Gradle project that has `hibernate-core`, `hibernate-entitymanager`, and `javax.persistence`.)

### 3.1  Persistence layer (entities & repositories)

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    private String name;
    private int age;

    /* ---------- standard getters/setters ---------- */
    // omitted for brevity
}

public interface UserRepository {
    User findById(Long id);
    User findByName(String name);
}
```

*Implementation of `UserRepository`* (just a thin wrapper around `EntityManager`):

```java
public class UserRepositoryImpl implements UserRepository {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public UserRepositoryImpl(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Override
    public User findById(Long id) {
        return em.find(User.class, id);
    }

    @Override
    public User findByName(String name) {
        TypedQuery<User> q = em.createQuery(
            "SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.name = :name", User.class);
        q.setParameter("name", name);
        return q.getSingleResult();
    }
}
```

### 3.2  The UnitOfWork implementation

```java
public class HibernateUnitOfWork implements UnitOfWork {

    private final EntityManager em;
    private final List<Object> newEntities   = new ArrayList<>();
    private final List<Object> dirtyEntities = new ArrayList<>();
    private final List<Object> deletedEntities = new ArrayList<>();

    public HibernateUnitOfWork(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Override
    public void registerNew(Object entity) {
        newEntities.add(entity);
    }

    @Override
    public void registerDirty(Object entity) {
        dirtyEntities.add(entity);
    }

    @Override
    public void registerDeleted(Object entity) {
        deletedEntities.add(entity);
    }

    @Override
    public void commit() throws PersistenceException {
        EntityTransaction tx = em.getTransaction();
        tx.begin();
        try {
            // 1️⃣ Persist new objects
            for (Object o : newEntities) {
                em.persist(o);
            }

            // 2️⃣ Merge dirty objects
            for (Object o : dirtyEntities) {
                em.merge(o);
            }

            // 3️⃣ Remove deleted objects
            for (Object o : deletedEntities) {
                // If the entity is detached, merge first
                Object managed = em.contains(o) ? o : em.merge(o);
                em.remove(managed);
            }

            tx.commit();   // all changes go out at once
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            if (tx.isActive()) tx.rollback();
            throw new PersistenceException("UnitOfWork failed", e);
        } finally {
            // Clear state so a new transaction can start cleanly
            newEntities.clear();
            dirtyEntities.clear();
            deletedEntities.clear();
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void rollback() {
        EntityTransaction tx = em.getTransaction();
        if (tx.isActive()) tx.rollback();
    }
}
```

> **Why `merge` for dirty objects?**  
> `merge` is safe whether the entity is attached or detached. In most real‑world code you would keep the entity attached in the same persistence context, but `merge` guarantees that even if it is not, the changes get written.

### 3.3  A small service that uses the UnitOfWork

```java
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository userRepo;
    private final UnitOfWork uow;

    public UserService(UserRepository userRepo, UnitOfWork uow) {
        this.userRepo = userRepo;
        this.uow = uow;
    }

    /**
     * Creates a new user and updates the age of an existing user
     * – both operations are part of the same UnitOfWork.
     */
    public void createUserAndUpgradeExisting(String newName, int newAge, Long existingUserId) {
        // 1️⃣ Build a new entity
        User newUser = new User();
        newUser.setName(newName);
        newUser.setAge(newAge);
        uow.registerNew(newUser);

        // 2️⃣ Load an existing entity and modify it
        User existing = userRepo.findById(existingUserId);
        existing.setAge(existing.getAge() + 1);   // just a demo change
        uow.registerDirty(existing);

        // 3️⃣ Commit everything in a single transaction
        uow.commit();
    }
}
```

### 3.4  Wiring it up (simple Java SE test)

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1️⃣ Create an EntityManagerFactory (normally you’d use a container)
        EntityManagerFactory emf = Persistence.createEntityManagerFactory("myPU");
        EntityManager em = emf.createEntityManager();

        // 2️⃣ Create components
        UserRepository repo = new UserRepositoryImpl(em);
        UnitOfWork uow = new HibernateUnitOfWork(em);
        UserService svc = new UserService(repo, uow);

        // 3️⃣ Perform the business operation
        svc.createUserAndUpgradeExisting("Alice", 30, 1L);

        // 4️⃣ Cleanup
        em.close();
        emf.close();
    }
}
```

> Replace `"myPU"` with a persistence unit name defined in `persistence.xml`.

---

## 4.  Why bother when Hibernate’s Session is already a UnitOfWork?

Yes, `Session` (or `EntityManager`) already keeps an **Identity Map**, tracks changes, and can flush a batch in a single commit.  
However, building a *thin, application‑level* UoW wrapper has practical advantages:

| Reason | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Framework agnosticism** | Your services talk to an interface (`UnitOfWork`). You can swap Hibernate for jOOQ, JDBC, or even a remote service without touching business code. |
| **Centralised transaction handling** | All commits go through the same path. You can add logging, auditing, metrics, or custom retry logic in one place. |
| **Explicit change registration** | In very large domains you may want to register only entities that actually changed. The wrapper gives you that visibility. |
| **Testability** | In unit tests you can inject a *mock* UnitOfWork that simply records what would have been persisted. |

---

## 5.  Advanced considerations

| Topic | Quick notes |
|-------|-------------|
| **Thread‑local UnitOfWork** | Often you want one UoW per request/thread. Use `ThreadLocal<UnitOfWork>` or a container‑provided `@RequestScoped` bean. |
| **Optimistic locking** | If you need to guard against lost updates, enable `@Version` in your entities. The commit will throw `OptimisticLockException` which you can catch and decide to retry or fail. |
| **Batch size** | `Session`/`EntityManager` can batch inserts/updates (`hibernate.jdbc.batch_size`). The UoW can expose a method to set this per transaction if desired. |
| **Lazy loading** | Be careful: accessing lazily loaded associations inside the UoW can trigger extra queries. Either initialize them beforehand or use fetch joins. |
| **Nested UoWs** | Some applications require a “super” UoW that encloses multiple sub‑operations. This can be modelled by stacking UoWs or using a *global* transaction manager (`UserTransaction`). |

---

## 6.  Recap

1. **Unit‑of‑Work** groups a set of changes to entities and commits them together.  
2. In Java you can implement it on top of JPA/Hibernate by keeping lists of new/dirty/deleted objects and delegating to `EntityManager`.  
3. A simple wrapper (`HibernateUnitOfWork`) lets you register changes, commit or roll back, and keeps the business code clean.  
4. Even though JPA already implements UoW, a custom layer gives you explicit control, easier testing, and framework independence.

> **Tip:** If you’re already using Spring, the same pattern is achieved with `@Transactional` and the `PlatformTransactionManager`. The UoW wrapper simply becomes the transaction demarcation point.

Happy coding! 🚀
